  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 119 PM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nAs of this afternoon, the heaviest rainfall has moved offshore, \nwhile lingering light showers/rain continues across portions of S \nTX. Models indicate additional light showers through the evening \nhours along with a few thunderstorms across the coastal waters.\n\nModels are in fair agreement with the mid level trough and surface \ntrough of low pressure tracking east overnight, and ending rain \nchances from west to east through Wednesday morning. In its wake, \ndrier mid level air is progged to filter across S TX while low \nlevel moisture will remain in place. Combined with light winds, \nmay allow for marginally efficient radiational fog to develop \nacross inland portions of S TX where there are breaks in the \nclouds. A mid level ridge of high pressure is forecast to build \nacross S TX through the remainder of the work week, maintaining \nvery low to nil rain chances. A dry cold front is expected to \nreach S TX by Thursday and stall. Lots of uncertainty as to where \nthe front stalls, but it will be the focus for rain over the \nweekend as an upper level trough approaches from the west. Not \nmuch cold air advection expected with this next cold front, but \nmay be most noticeable Thursday and Friday nights with lows in the\nmid 50s to low 60s. Overall, expecting temps to be near to \nslightly above normal through the period.\n\nMinor coastal flooding is not expected tonight, but could be close \nas tides will remain elevated through the next high tide this \nafternoon/evening. Will continue to monitor trends for any tides \nthat are higher than currently expected.",
